1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,040
Now we turn to partnership with school.

2
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,600
Keep that home observation in mind.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,200
Your child, your family and the school

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:13,760
may each hold different parts of the
picture.

5
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:17,560
Bringing those together can help shape
useful support.

6
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:20,000
People arrive with very different school
experiences.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,560
Some relationships feel helpful,

8
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:23,680
and others have been difficult.

9
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:26,160
You can take part without sharing a
personal account.

10
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,680
Please keep staff, schools and children

11
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:30,860
unidentifiable in group discussion.

12
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:36,810
Look at the overlapping partnerships:
family, school staff and support

13
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:39,320
staff, with your child at the centre.

14
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:43,680
Each person brings different knowledge and
a different relationship.

15
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:47,960
Who else belongs in that picture for your
family: perhaps a grandparent,

16
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,680
therapist or out-of-school-hours carer?

17
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,680
What is the shared purpose of these
partnerships?

18
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,680
How can your child's own communication
help shape it?

19
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:02,680
Take a moment to reflect or discuss, and
pause if you'd like more time.

20
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,960
Keep your child at the centre as you
consider the answer.

21
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,760
No single person holds the whole picture.

22
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:20,680
A useful partnership helps people
understand each other's knowledge

23
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:23,640
and agree on support that makes a
difference to the child.

24
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:29,150
The Family-School Partnerships Framework
recognises parents and

25
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:32,520
families as children's first and
continuing educators.

26
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:36,520
Learning happens across settings, and a
family's diversity and strengths

27
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:38,080
matter to that learning.

28
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:42,190
The other principles include community
involvement, mutual trust and

29
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:43,520
shared responsibility,

30
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:46,320
supported by committed and collaborative
leadership.

31
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:50,890
In an everyday conversation, look for
respectful listening, clear

32
00:01:50,890 --> 00:01:54,960
information, your child's views and agreed
next steps.

33
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:58,750
Family knowledge and school knowledge are
both needed from the

34
00:01:58,750 --> 00:01:59,520
beginning.

35
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:02,660
Respectful listening is possible even when
people see a situation

36
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:03,520
differently.

37
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:08,960
What makes collaboration work?

38
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:12,000
Think of a situation where people have
worked well together,

39
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
inside or outside school.

40
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,200
What helped them listen and make a
decision?

41
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,480
Share a brief idea or reflect privately.

42
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:23,400
Pause here if you would like time.

43
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:36,510
The slide brings together shared goals and
values, mutual trust,

44
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:38,440
consultation, curiosity

45
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,880
and attention to different perspectives.

46
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,320
Your child's voice belongs throughout.

47
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:48,060
The phrase assume good intent may feel
difficult if trust has been

48
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:49,040
damaged. Trust

49
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:53,440
needs action from both sides, and it can
take time to rebuild.

50
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,980
A possible opening is: how could we work
together so school goes well

51
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:59,160
for my child?

52
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:02,900
That gives the conversation a purpose
while leaving room to discuss

53
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:04,120
what needs to change.

54
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:10,200
There are also responsibilities that sit
behind these conversations.

55
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:14,250
The Disability Discrimination Act protects
people from discrimination

56
00:03:14,250 --> 00:03:15,920
because of disability,

57
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,120
including in education.

58
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:21,610
The Disability Standards for Education
explain obligations to support

59
00:03:21,610 --> 00:03:22,840
access and participation

60
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:27,320
on the same basis as students without
disability.

61
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:31,420
Schools must consult the student, or an
associate such as a parent or

62
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:32,720
carer, when considering

63
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,480
reasonable adjustments.

64
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:38,800
Useful questions are: how will we be
consulted?

65
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:40,760
What adjustments are in place?

66
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:44,400
How and when will they be reviewed?

67
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:48,750
This is general information, rather than
advice about an individual

68
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:50,120
legal situation.

69
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:53,470
The Department of Education's
plain-language resources explain these

70
00:03:53,470 --> 00:03:54,840
rights and responsibilities

71
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:55,840
for families.

72
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:59,240
They are a useful place to return to after
the workshop.

73
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:06,350
Power can sit in the way a meeting is
arranged, the language used and

74
00:04:06,350 --> 00:04:07,400
who can make decisions.

75
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:12,840
Familiarity with school systems, cultural
and historical experiences of

76
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:13,880
education,

77
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:17,560
language and financial circumstances can
also shape how a conversation

78
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:17,960
feels.

79
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:23,400
Who sets the agenda? Whose knowledge gets
heard? Is your child included

80
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:24,680
in a way that works for them?

81
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:27,720
What might need to change about the
process?

82
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:32,040
Naming these features can help us plan a
more balanced conversation.

83
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:36,040
We can examine the meeting itself without
turning the discussion into a

84
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,680
judgement about an identifiable person.

85
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:46,080
Use the Power dynamics activity on page
fifteen, or three sticky notes.

86
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:49,560
First, describe an imbalance from a home
perspective.

87
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:52,480
On the second, consider one from a school
perspective.

88
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:56,880
On the third, suggest how the imbalance
could be addressed.

89
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:00,120
Thinking about another perspective doesn't
mean excusing poor practice.

90
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:04,080
It helps us identify what could change in
a conversation.

91
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:08,400
You can share at your table, add anonymous
notes under Home, School

92
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,680
and Solutions, or keep your notes private.

93
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:12,960
Pause now for the activity.

94
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,320
Look again at the solutions. Could the
agenda be shared earlier?

95
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,160
Could language be clearer,

96
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:28,280
a support person be included,

97
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:29,840
or another way of contributing

98
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,360
be available?

99
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:35,040
Choose one idea that would make
participation more workable.

100
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:37,280
Keep it for the conversation plan later in
this session.

101
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:40,200
We're going to hear from Adon.

102
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:44,780
As you watch, pay attention to what
decisions were made and who made

103
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:45,120
them.

104
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:50,180
Notice the effect of being included, or
not included, in decisions about

105
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:51,560
your own experience.

106
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:50,160
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon?

107
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:53,880
Make a note on page fifteen, or discuss
the question briefly.

108
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:59,360
Then consider how your child's preferences
are asked for and heard.

109
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:00,800
Pause here if you'd like more time.

110
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:12,120
Children can have a say through the ways
they communicate:

111
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:17,080
words, drawings, typing, visual choices,
gesture or other responses.

112
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:21,050
We can ask what helps, what is hard and
what they want a teacher to

113
00:08:21,050 --> 00:08:21,720
know.

114
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:24,990
Their own perspective belongs in the
conversation, including when it

115
00:08:24,990 --> 00:08:27,160
differs from what adults expected.

116
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:32,040
Turn to What works on page sixteen.

117
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:34,080
Think of a time you worked well with
someone at school.

118
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:35,600
What made it work?

119
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:38,800
What did they do, and what did you do?

120
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:40,920
If a school example is difficult to find,

121
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:43,080
choose another helpful relationship

122
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,080
or listen to ideas from others.

123
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:47,600
Talk at your table without naming staff or
schools,

124
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:50,320
and note anything worth carrying forward.

125
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:51,800
Pause for that reflection.

126
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:04,560
The practical moves on the slide are
useful starting points:

127
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:09,720
prepare, share information, ask questions
and seek clarification.

128
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:13,420
You can ask for an agenda beforehand,
arrange to bring a support person

129
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:15,360
and bring your own notes.

130
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:19,810
Ask for a record of agreed actions, then
follow up in writing so

131
00:09:19,810 --> 00:09:22,320
everyone can check what was understood.

132
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:26,770
If trust is strained, a clear purpose and
a small agreed step may be a

133
00:09:26,770 --> 00:09:27,520
beginning.

134
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:33,970
Consultation is more useful when there is
a clear shared purpose, open

135
00:09:33,970 --> 00:09:36,520
questions and more than one way to
contribute.

136
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:40,520
Your knowledge and your child's knowledge
deserve attention.

137
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:45,040
What was heard needs to be recorded
accurately, followed by an agreed

138
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:47,520
action, responsibility and follow-up.

139
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:52,520
The checklist on page sixteen can help
before and after a meeting.

140
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:55,520
Ask, listen and follow up.

141
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:58,990
Before leaving, check who will do what and
when you will hear how it is

142
00:09:58,990 --> 00:09:59,520
going.

143
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:03,960
A conversation becomes more useful when
everyone understands the next

144
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:04,520
step.

145
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:10,450
The Working Together Communication Tool,
on the left, helps you agree how

146
00:10:10,450 --> 00:10:11,800
home and school will communicate.

147
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:16,600
You can choose a method, such as email,
phone or a communication book,

148
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:21,000
then record how often, what you'll
communicate about, and the next dates

149
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:21,780
or steps.

150
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:25,800
The Partnership Planning Template, on the
right, asks what has worked,

151
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:28,260
what has been challenging and what could
help.

152
00:10:28,260 --> 00:10:32,020
You can use those prompts before a meeting
or complete them together.

153
00:10:32,020 --> 00:10:34,820
A tool gives the conversation a place to
start.

154
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:37,780
Keep listening and your child's own
communication alongside it.

155
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:44,120
The N C C D Meetings Tool Kit is a free
resource for families and schools

156
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:45,560
preparing for meetings

157
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:47,360
about adjustments.

158
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:50,560
It covers before, during and after a
meeting.

159
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:53,960
The link is in the resources at the back
of your workbook.

160
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:57,160
You can use it to think about what
information to bring and what to

161
00:10:57,160 --> 00:10:58,160
clarify.

162
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:02,760
Your school may have its own process, so
ask how consultation and

163
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:04,960
follow-up will work in your setting.

164
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:09,280
Help is available beyond this workshop.

165
00:11:09,280 --> 00:11:13,120
Autism Connect offers autism information
and support.

166
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:16,560
Disability Gateway helps people find
disability information

167
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:18,280
and services. Carer

168
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:21,000
Gateway provides support for carers.

169
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,160
Their contact details are at the back of
your workbook.

170
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:28,680
Lifeline's crisis support number is
thirteen, eleven, fourteen.

171
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:31,800
Kids Helpline provides counselling for
children

172
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:39,000
and young people aged five to twenty-five
on eighteen hundred, fifty-five, eighteen
hundred.

173
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,960
Its website also has information for
parents and carers,

174
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:47,840
and points parents towards Parentline for
parenting counselling.

175
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:50,880
Take a moment to note the support that is
relevant to you.

176
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:53,880
You don't have to work everything out by
yourself.

177
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,400
Turn to page seventeen and choose one
purposeful conversation with school.

178
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:05,600
What needs to be understood?

179
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:08,400
What question could open the conversation?

180
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:11,400
How will your child's views be included?

181
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:15,000
What could be decided together, and how
will you follow up?

182
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Use short notes.

183
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,740
Bring in one idea from the power activity
that could make the

184
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:21,000
conversation more balanced.

185
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
There is no need to share a sensitive
situation.

186
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
Pause here and give yourself time to
prepare.

187
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:37,880
Circle the question you want to open with.

188
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:39,720
Keep these notes for Session Six.

189
00:12:39,720 --> 00:12:43,640
A small, specific next step is enough:

190
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,400
one thing that needs to be understood,

191
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:47,560
and a way to hear from your child as part
of it.
